---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Drip vs Postscript for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = "For a Shopify brand already using Drip, the immediate question may be whether to add Postscript SMS. There is also a newer replacement question: Postscript advertises email with cohort-based access. A launch page is evidence of an offering, not confirmation that your store can migrate tomorrow.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Postscript still SMS only?",
    "answer": "No. Postscript advertises Email, including joint channel flows, but says access is opened in cohorts. Confirm availability for your store."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I replace Drip immediately?",
    "answer": "Do not assume so. Obtain email access and validate the required workflows, suppression history and migration scope first."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Drip and Postscript coexist?",
    "answer": "Yes as an architecture choice, provided channel permissions and cross-system exclusions have a documented implementation and owner. This is not a claim of a native connector."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should the trial prove?",
    "answer": "For this restock example, prove consent handling, purchase exits, reply ownership and sold-out behavior before comparing conversion or spend."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Postscript for Shopify</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">For a Shopify brand already using Drip, the immediate question may be whether to add Postscript SMS. There is also a newer replacement question: Postscript advertises email with cohort-based access. A launch page is evidence of an offering, not confirmation that your store can migrate tomorrow.</p></header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Adding text now while evaluating Postscript Email access</h2>
    <p>This comparison is an editorial evaluation framework, checked against primary product information on 24 November 2026. The exercises below are proposed tests; no merchant trial or performance result is claimed.</p>
    <h2>Correct the SMS-only assumption</h2>
    <p>Postscript now describes email campaigns, automation branches and combined SMS/email flows. Its email FAQ says access opens in cohorts after joining the list. Keep those two facts together: dismissing its email entirely is outdated, while assuming immediate availability is also unsafe. Ask for an account-specific access date and supported migration scope before planning a Drip cancellation.</p>
    <h2>Proposed workflow: limited-stock restock alert</h2>
    <p>Use a hypothetical store selling a limited restock. An email subscriber on Drip requests a text alert on the product page. Record the SMS opt-in through the actual approved collection flow in Postscript; do not convert a Drip interest tag into consent. Store the product interest separately from channel permission. Have an internal tester request alerts for two products and withdraw text permission before either restocks. Verify that interest can persist without authorizing a text.</p>
    <h2>Prevent a restock and cart collision</h2>
    <p>For a second internal tester, send the restock invitation, add the item to a cart and then purchase before a planned reminder. Inspect which Postscript automation and which Drip workflow remain queued. Assign one owner to resolve overlapping restock, cart and winback paths. If an exclusion crosses systems, document its transport, expected delay, retries and what happens during an outage. Do not invent a native Drip-to-Postscript tag sync or rely on a nightly file for urgent suppression.</p>
    <h2>Replies and launch inventory</h2>
    <p>Ask Postscript to demonstrate where shopper replies are handled and who can pause the restock automation. Define a process for a customer asking about size or stock so the response does not become another automated promotion. Rehearse a product selling out between message composition and click: verify the landing page and any fallback offer. These checks distinguish a restock program from a generic welcome-email comparison and are proposed exercises, not observed recovery results.</p>
    <h2>Email replacement requires a separate acceptance pass</h2>
    <p>If Postscript Email becomes available to your store, rebuild an existing Drip welcome branch, a cart exit rule and a post-purchase sequence in the offered product. Check template portability, unsubscribe history, sending-domain setup and historical reporting requirements. A combined flow could remove inter-provider handoffs, but the team still needs to inspect channel eligibility. Keep the existing Drip email program until the replacement scope and cutover behavior have been accepted.</p>
    <h2>Budget by messages and channel access</h2>
    <p>Use Postscript pricing for the exact package and ask whether email is included or separately contracted. Obtain the SMS/MMS destination, carrier and message-segment assumptions; a long text can change the budget. Model a small targeted restock and a full eligible-list launch separately. Add Drip active-person and email-volume costs if email stays there. Do not infer the combined bill from a single contact count or a claimed typical merchant price.</p>
    <h2>Measure an addition without double-counting</h2>
    <p>For a proposed experiment, compare eligible audiences with a pre-agreed holdout if feasible and suitable for your program. Record Shopify orders, discounts, product margin, returns and messaging cost separately from vendor attribution. A text that follows an email can receive credit for an order the email would have produced. Use the comparison to decide whether the added channel covers its operational cost, rather than requiring an arbitrary share of retention revenue.</p>
    <h2>Cutover and fallback</h2>
    <p>Launch SMS to an internal test segment first, then a deliberately limited eligible audience. Record which cart, restock and winback paths are live in each provider. If access to Postscript Email is pending, that is a dependency in the plan, not a reason to rush an email migration. If cross-system suppression cannot be made reliable, reduce overlapping automations until the team resolves it. Choose the architecture from demonstrated behavior and available scope.</p>
    <h2>Primary sources and limits</h2><ul>
      <li><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">Postscript Email and access FAQ</a></li>
      <li><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/pricing">Postscript package and usage pricing</a></li>
      <li><a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695491725-Connect-Your-Shopify-Store">Drip Shopify connection</a></li>
      <li><a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/pricing">Drip current pricing</a></li>
    </ul><p>Product documentation establishes advertised scope. Account access, integration latency and commercial terms still require confirmation for the proposed setup. No runtime tests were performed for this comparison.</p>
    </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
